{
  "an3.39:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 3.39 ",
  "an3.39:0.2": "4. Messengers of the Gods ",
  "an3.39:0.3": "A Delicate Lifestyle ",
  "an3.39:1.1": "“My lifestyle was delicate, mendicants, most delicate, extremely delicate. ",
  "an3.39:1.2": "In my father’s home, lotus ponds were made just for me. ",
  "an3.39:1.3": "In some, blue water lilies blossomed, while in others, there were pink or white lotuses, just for my benefit. ",
  "an3.39:1.4": "I only used sandalwood from Kāsi, and my turbans, jackets, sarongs, and upper robes also came from Kāsi. ",
  "an3.39:1.5": "And a white parasol was held over me night and day, with the thought: ",
  "an3.39:1.6": "‘Don’t let cold, heat, grass, dust, or damp bother him.’ ",
  "an3.39:2.1": "I had three stilt longhouses—one for the winter, one for the summer, and one for the rainy season. ",
  "an3.39:2.2": "I stayed in a stilt longhouse without coming downstairs for the four months of the rainy season, where I was entertained by musicians—none of them men. ",
  "an3.39:2.3": "While the bondservants, workers, and staff in other houses are given sour gruel with false black pepper to eat, in my father’s home they are given fine rice with meat. ",
  "an3.39:3.1": "Amid such prosperity and such a delicate lifestyle, I thought: ",
  "an3.39:3.2": "‘When an unlearned ordinary person—who is liable to grow old, not being exempt from old age—sees someone else who is old, they’re horrified, repelled, and disgusted, overlooking the fact that they themsel